      <section class="project-section alt-bg">
        <div class="inner">
          <div class="project-header">
            <span class="section-tag">Project 02 · Audio DSP &amp; Immersive Research</span>
            <h2>ORBIT — Immersive Binaural Mixer for NDH 30, AI-assisted research</h2>
            <p class="role-statement">
              <strong>My role:</strong> Independent researcher / developer, personal learning tool
            </p>
            <p class="project-status">
              <strong>Status:</strong> Research in progress, v1.1, UI currently being rebuilt. Not a
              finished commercial plugin, not distributed.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="project-body">
            <div class="reflective-block">
              <h3>Reflective Overview &amp; Technical Methodology</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>ORBIT</strong> (Immersive Binaural Mixer for NDH 30, AI-assisted research —
                part of the <em>Immersive Binaural Tools Research and Development with AI</em>
                project) is a personal, independent research tool engineered specifically for my own
                workflow, created to learn and master immersive mixing “in the box” using my Neumann
                NDH 30 open-back reference headphones.
              </p>
              <p>
                The core research objective was twofold: investigate how artificial intelligence can
                accelerate the mathematical and structural design of novel audio plugins, and explore
                reverse engineering with AI as an advanced learning methodology by rigorously
                analyzing complex, functional reference architectures.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Strict Clean-Room Methodology:</strong> The reverse engineering approach was
                applied strictly and exclusively to open-source reference plugins (notably the IEM
                Plug-in Suite and SPARTA), with licenses thoroughly verified and attributed.
                Absolutely zero code was copied from any commercial plugin. No proprietary code,
                closed-source FIR correction curve, or decompiled binary data from any commercial
                product was extracted or used.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Headphone Calibration:</strong> Headphone acoustic compensation is derived
                entirely from public measurement data published by Oratory1990 for the Neumann NDH 30.
                This is an open, community-accessible measurement dataset, rather than any
                manufacturer-proprietary correction profile. The calibration curve is tailored
                specifically for my own reference headphones and listening chain.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Multi-Channel Architecture &amp; Performance:</strong> ORBIT supports
                comprehensive immersive monitor mixing formats: Stereo, 5.0, 7.0, 7.1, 7.0.4, 7.1.2,
                and 7.1.4. The engine features FIR convolution, spatial HRTF / binaural rendering,
                SOFA dataset loading, personalized ITD (Interaural Time Difference) calculation, and
                dynamic real-time head tracking via OSC (Yaw, Pitch, Roll). Built in C++ using the
                JUCE framework as both Standalone and VST3 formats, the DSP convolution pipeline is
                heavily accelerated with Apple vDSP vector instructions on Apple Silicon (M1 Pro).
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Academic Value for Master's Studies:</strong> This project consolidates
                essential domains of advanced acoustic and audio engineering: real-time digital signal
                processing (DSP), high-order FIR convolution, spatialized HRTF synthesis,
                object-based multi-channel routing, and AI-assisted software architecture.
              </p>
              <p class="disclaimer-text">
                <em>
                  Independent Research Notice: ORBIT is an independent academic learning project. It
                  is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or a copy of any commercial hardware or
                  software manufacturer, and is not distributed commercially.
                </em>
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Tech Badges -->
